# Subject line: one line, imperative mood, roughly 72 characters or less.


# What changed and why. A few sentences is plenty — write for whoever is
# bisecting this six months from now.


# Operational notes — anything a human has to do before or after this deploys:
#   - settings to change in QuickBooks, Stripe, or Salesforce
#   - ordering constraints ("deploy this before/after X")
#   - jobs, scripts, or backfills to run
#   - "this does nothing until X exists"
# Write "None." when there genuinely are none, so an empty section is
# deliberate rather than forgotten.
#
# Never put a credential, token, or other secret value here. Commit messages
# are permanent and readable by anyone with access to the repository.

Operational notes:

# Remember: the merge commit is what survives, not the pull request discussion.
# GitHub's default merge commit records only "Merge pull request #N from
# owner/branch" and keeps none of the above. Squash-merge instead, and keep the
# PR body as the squash commit message so this text reaches the history.
#
# Lines starting with '#' are stripped by git.
